{
  "about": "What a package store in any language must do with a pinned archive. Each case starts with an empty store under the case's limits (maxArchiveBytes, maxTreeFiles, maxTreeBytes, maxArtifactBytes; a limit that is absent is the store's default) and runs its steps in order. An 'install' step installs or upgrades from the archive file it names, relative to this file, with the pinned 'sha256' (hexadecimal in either case; an empty string is a missing digest) and, when 'id' is present, the package ID the archive must hold; a file that does not exist ('archives/absent.bin') must never be opened, which is how a case shows that a receipt made the store skip the archive. A 'receipt' step reads the install receipt of a package and expects its 'revision' and, as 'sha256', the lower-case digest of the archive that produced it. A 'remove' step removes a package. A 'damage' step overwrites the file 'path' of the installed package, in the directory named by its ID with every '/' written '%2f', with the seven bytes 'damaged'. Every step expects 'status' (ok when absent): ok, invalid, mismatch, unsupported, not_found or exists. A successful install also expects the installed package's 'revision'. The format is found from the content: a zip starts 'PK\\x03\\x04' or 'PK\\x05\\x06', a gzip-compressed tar starts 0x1f 0x8b, a plain tar has 'ustar' at offset 257; anything else is unsupported. Only directories and regular files are extracted; a tar global header is skipped. The manifest, plugin.json, is at the top of the archive or in its one top-level directory. Each entry, directory or file, counts toward maxTreeFiles; the bytes of files toward maxTreeBytes; one file toward maxArtifactBytes; the archive file toward maxArchiveBytes. After every case nothing is left under the store root but package directories and '.receipts', and no case may create /tmp/ext-archive-escape.",
